The provided code snippet is a C# class named `TrickplayManager` that implements the `ITrickplayManager` interface. It has several methods for managing trickplay data, including `MoveGeneratedTrickplayDataAsync` and `RefreshTrickplayDataAsync`. The `RefreshTrickplayDataAsync` method is of particular interest because it calls the `LogInformation` method on the `_logger` object, which is an instance of `ILogger<TrickplayManager>`.

To write unit tests for this class, we can use a testing framework like xUnit. We can create test cases for the `MoveGeneratedTrickplayDataAsync` and `RefreshTrickplayDataAsync` methods to ensure they behave as expected.

Here's an example of how we might write a test for the `RefreshTrickplayDataAsync` method:
```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

namespace Jellyfin.Server.Implementations.Trickplay
{
    public class TrickplayManagerTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public async Task RefreshTrickplayDataAsync_LogsInformation_WhenTrickplayFilesAreCreated()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<TrickplayManager>>();
            var trickplayManager = new TrickplayManager(
                loggerMock.Object,
                new Mock<IMediaEncoder>().Object,
                new Mock<IFileSystem>().Object,
                new Mock<EncodingHelper>().Object,
                new Mock<IServerConfigurationManager>().Object,
                new Mock<IImageEncoder>().Object,
                new Mock<IDbContextFactory<JellyfinDbContext>>().Object,
                new Mock<IApplicationPaths>().Object,
                new Mock<IPathManager>().Object);

            // Act
            await trickplayManager.RefreshTrickplayDataAsync(
                new Video { Id = "videoId", Name = "videoName" },
                true,
                new LibraryOptions { EnableTrickplayImageExtraction = true, SaveTrickplayWithMedia = true },
                CancellationToken.None);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogInformation(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```
This test case creates a mock `ILogger<TrickplayManager>` object and passes it to the `TrickplayManager` constructor. It then calls the `RefreshTrickplayDataAsync` method and verifies that the `LogInformation` method was called on the mock logger object.

Note that this test case assumes that the `RefreshTrickplayDataAsync` method will call the `LogInformation` method when trickplay files are created. If this is not the case, the test may need to be modified accordingly.

Also, the test case uses Moq to create mock objects for the dependencies of the `TrickplayManager` class. This allows us to isolate the `TrickplayManager` class and test its behavior in isolation.

